#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084269} :activity/id #uuid "b5963314-027e-58a8-94c3-2b38175169a4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198 :topic/id #uuid "35c03b2e-e860-5cb2-96d6-2e103a19283f" :topic/math-academy-id 788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084482 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ is normally distributed. Then the probability density function $f(x)$ of $X$ is a unimodal, bell-shaped curve. Moreover, it is symmetrical about the mean, median, and mode, which all equal $\\mu$.  \n\n![](images/0c/0c8951446f52cea9db6c0a2b600fc53cb564c3d64ff0afab15c9756dc0c0f29b.png)\n\nWe can use the normal distribution to model a wide range of real-life situations because many real-world distributions are roughly bell-shaped and symmetrical around a mean value. \n\nPatients' blood pressures, people's weights, measurement errors, and IQ scores are just some of the real-world examples that can be modeled as a normal distribution. \n\nTo determine whether a given data is normal, the first thing to do is to check the shape of the distribution. For example, if a data set is roughly bell-shaped, then it *might* be normally distributed. \n\nIt should be noted that, in practice, statisticians will often need to perform several tests on the data to check for normality (these are called **normality tests**). We cannot rely only on a distribution's shape since other distributions are also bell-shaped. However, checking the shape is often a good starting point." :tutorial/id #uuid "9f150bce-c1b4-502c-bcb1-da067550b958" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4966 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "932c4862-d05e-5b90-85c8-d54c3a5130a8" :step/math-academy-id 30927 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083126 :question/id #uuid "cdd74ee4-b82f-520f-8541-f328f9e63fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8279"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493971}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"} :step/id #uuid "37a09439-27ba-50b5-9e58-473fafe4e323" :step/math-academy-id 30928 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083127 :question/id #uuid "5905ffa9-e7e2-5288-8714-91357fb17139" :question/math-academy-id "e-8280"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507444}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"} :step/id #uuid "fd4c06eb-e846-5f38-b100-a7f13c969c43" :step/math-academy-id 30929 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083128 :question/id #uuid "4049eb36-a8b0-5ef7-bf82-6f38cbe5cb17" :question/math-academy-id "e-8281"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507489}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"} :step/id #uuid "828a2000-0cb2-577a-9a4c-50d319487a43" :step/math-academy-id 30930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083342 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083145 :question/id #uuid "c55db5a3-2d8c-57d7-b860-8628cb8738f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9892"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048615}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493946}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"} :step/id #uuid "98535c60-8311-53a8-9f73-e175c3ec6497" :step/math-academy-id 37200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084270}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083466}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415883} :activity/id #uuid "2c1f6cc5-5968-538f-9311-8522a9dd360b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198 :topic/id #uuid "35c03b2e-e860-5cb2-96d6-2e103a19283f" :topic/math-academy-id 788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$9$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415873} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef719c3-66a7-50b4-a2f7-e921efeb034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415874} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4b0043-0c74-53a6-a993-6328a52b0af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415875} {:answer/id #uuid "58d37435-1075-579f-a1f9-ce0f3925f1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$11$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415876} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fee109-4588-538f-b649-9c3fd53fe5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415878}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415873} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b712d39-ff45-54f8-b106-03be0d0cda60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "979b0f60-49c9-5b48-86f8-6e501f45d940" :question/math-academy-id "q-95151" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the videos produced by a popular content creator is $35\\,\\text{min}.$ Also, $5%$ of their videos have a duration of less than $20$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of the videos is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the duration times, rounded to the near minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a video. Then we have $X∼N(35,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 20)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-35}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{15}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1097 \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈9\\,\\text{minutes}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "08ba30f8-c04b-524e-80f7-0ec881803edf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce3962b-9372-53e8-94dd-19aae1e9b687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300674} :answer-field/id #uuid "752c40b7-22f4-5848-b34a-2e00b4e7dbd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6829b460-7921-509d-b187-5e5360672369" :question/math-academy-id "q-324314" :question/problem "The lengths of metal rods produced by a certain factory are normally distributed with a mean of $100$ centimeters and a standard deviation of $4$ centimeters. What is the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the rod length (in centimeters). Then we have $X∼N(100,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(96.8\\le X<102.4).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(96.8\\le X<102.4) & =P(\\frac{96.8-100}{4}\\le Z<\\frac{102.4-100}{4}) \\\\  & =P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60) & =P(Z<0.60)-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.60)-P(Z\\le -0.80) \\\\  & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.60) & =0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.80) & =0.2119.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80<Z<0.60) & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =0.7257-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.5138 \\\\  & ≈0.51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately $0.51.$"} :step/id #uuid "13582afe-72fb-5625-8464-5f459b09da11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415877}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415877} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ee986dc-58c5-5a6b-9a45-c8daf6a2b863" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "384a66a5-a5c9-58f8-aa5d-723b50b59cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325130" :question/problem "The fasting glucose level of a clinic’s patients is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and a standard deviation of $15\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Additionally, $60%$ of the patients have a glucose level between $0.92\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and $1.08\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Find the percentage of patients with a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ | $\\,0.0314\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ | $\\,0.0392\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the fasting glucose level of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<130).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.08\\mu  & =12.624 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{12.624}{0.08} \\\\  & ≈157.80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<130)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<130) & =P(Z<\\frac{130-157.80}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.85) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.85) \\\\  & =0.0322 \\\\  & ≈3.22%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.22%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$"} :step/id #uuid "4bf19974-65f5-541f-96e4-687af8d2f431"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fb6f7f4-57a5-52f7-8e64-395b91ab0e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300689} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc161ec3-c1d9-5eb1-baaa-2ab67d18a5ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aace1ce7-1675-5a50-8ee4-f15b9647304c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324349" :question/problem "The flight times of a particular drone model are normally distributed with a mean of $35$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge? **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the drone flight time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(35,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>40.08).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>40.08) & =P(Z>\\frac{40.08-35}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.27)=1-0.8980≈0.10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately $0.10.$"} :step/id #uuid "b1290537-191a-5941-898f-4165f87ba9c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209a5dc2-51b8-5cf8-a3fd-432567049203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266433} {:answer/id #uuid "285a1d46-eca2-5392-87b2-9ae6a067d406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266502} {:answer/id #uuid "383eb090-4034-58c4-90ea-549e44d474a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266633} {:answer/id #uuid "7be87196-7809-5fb8-a83b-62481b462d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267243} {:answer/id #uuid "bc16961d-f939-5b46-a020-4871a6a9d710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266502} :answer-field/id #uuid "0642bb25-91a1-5589-b640-3a5b72872474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "763a853d-691e-5772-a9d4-a4d4d0b3eb60" :question/math-academy-id "q-133053" :question/problem "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter."} :step/id #uuid "cd1c27a9-4fc5-505a-a0b1-c103e40a7537" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415884}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415872}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507553} :activity/id #uuid "2d2487a3-1789-5b3f-bcb6-be692fcece6c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198 :topic/id #uuid "35c03b2e-e860-5cb2-96d6-2e103a19283f" :topic/math-academy-id 788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f04dd09-9af6-5af5-b2a7-02412d51615e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082776} {:answer/id #uuid "95a31260-f0cc-565e-b971-d2bd4d135ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082777} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2a8b1e-34b7-5bdb-960e-a7263f101f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082778} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0255ec-0e04-54e7-ae22-0d510b62b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082779} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac1477f-55e1-52bb-abe7-4f577c033faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082776} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf9b764-d015-5366-9df6-e11472a54a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9debd84a-b5d9-54c6-84fa-986684bdfc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94702" :question/problem "In a factory, cookies contain $15$ grams of sugar on average. However, $10%$ of cookies contain more than $17$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the percent of cookies that contain fewer than $12$ grams of sugar, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution, *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of grams of sugar in a cookie. Then we have $X∼N(15,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>17)=0.1.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =1-P(X>17) \\\\  & =1-0.1 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =0.9 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{17-15}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)≈1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.6408 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.6408} \\\\  & ≈1.5605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12) & =P(X\\le 12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12-15}{1.5605}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.92) \\\\  & =0.0274 \\\\  & ≈2.7%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $2.7%$ of cookies contain less than $12$ grams of sugar."} :step/id #uuid "5bce04ca-70c6-54fe-8fc6-e22eeb0734f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cb42038-d4e2-5cd2-8193-28bac637ba07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082782} {:answer/id #uuid "292516a9-9b55-5825-97e9-ce95b5702014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082783} {:answer/id #uuid "3b79c1d9-f7d4-5316-be66-a07d9bb8fe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082784} {:answer/id #uuid "698291c0-9ecf-5cf7-aa1b-9afe2e390f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082785} {:answer/id #uuid "6aede979-f402-5d68-8ab7-96a5935c49a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082785} :answer-field/id #uuid "d097420f-f8f8-569f-a975-5e227fecbe9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e774eae-3ccc-5f1e-a35b-4ce8e1ddec45" :question/math-academy-id "q-94971" :question/problem "The test scores of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $5.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected student got a score of $80$ or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the score of a randomly selected student. Then we have $X∼N(73,{5}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 80).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 80) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{80-73}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1.4) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.4) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.40)=1-0.9192≈0.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student got $80$ or more is $0.08.$"} :step/id #uuid "b7e8932f-d2fc-59f3-abbd-cd780ac112de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c15084-f362-505f-8950-6df3b2548ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.74$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400677} {:answer/id #uuid "e01f491c-202f-5132-a3f5-3f0f2930aa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.65$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400678} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bf2-0abb-5dd3-8132-52b2488943a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.57$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400679} {:answer/id #uuid "7b383bc1-6fb9-59e8-b057-fb6dccdf74a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.79$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb566678-fdf6-552d-aad6-4bf9b9328ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132852" :question/problem "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c58cd6d2-7a47-5d68-ba83-ba5b2c86397f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d935e86d-2878-5713-88af-732c2cd4dd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb3c213e-a427-5f30-8b6a-3f5985900ded" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "62a29958-3d43-5575-a73a-6517a1734f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-324461" :question/problem "The price of a deli sandwich is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$1.10.$ In addition, in $90%$ of cases the price is between $$0.80\\mu $ and $$1.20\\mu .$ Find the average price, **rounding your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$dollars$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a sandwich price. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{1.10}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu )=0.90.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)=1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.20\\mu  & =1.809\\,39 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.809\\,39}{0.20} \\\\  & ≈9.05\\,\\text{dollars}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de38d27b-caa8-5826-b9f1-c99bfdbf34b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea413de4-f41d-5bd6-8d46-90d7116bd177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "51.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe2d838-2efe-5d60-8dd7-942ce7ac6cc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd63f548-8bbe-5de7-9356-fc14587f7fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-325117" :question/problem "The heights of oak trees in a park are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ feet and a standard deviation of $8$ feet. Additionally, $25%$ of the trees are shorter than $45$ feet. Find the percentage of trees taller than $50$ feet. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ | $\\,0.4404\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4880\\,$ | $\\,0.4840\\,$ | $\\,0.4801\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet."} :step/id #uuid "fadeb461-5bd5-527d-84af-be4656af31e5"}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506665}]}
